// What a rule can know about its neighbours.
//
// A context-sensitive production already asked *what stands beside this module* -- that is how the
// bacterium in `context-sensitive` passes a signal along its filament.  What it could not ask was
// **what are they carrying**.  Now it can:
//
//     '!(a)<!(c)>!(b)' -> '!(c + 0.25 * (a + b - 2 * c))'
//
// Each cell works out its new value from the two either side of it, and that expression is the
// discrete diffusion equation: a cell moves a quarter of the way toward the average of its
// neighbours.  There is no way to write it without parameters in the context -- knowing that a
// neighbour is *there* tells you nothing about how much it holds.
//
// The five rods are the same filament after nought, two, six, twelve and twenty steps, from the far
// side to the near.  Every one begins as a thin thread with a single fat cell in the middle; each
// step spreads that bulge a little further and lowers it a little, until it is a long shallow swell.
// The material is the same throughout and the segment lengths never change, so the shape you are
// looking at is nothing but the numbers being passed from cell to cell.
//
// **`!` takes the width to draw at**, so a cell's number is its own thickness -- which is why the
// signal can simply be looked at rather than plotted.
//
// **The `F` between cells is ignored for context.**  Without `ignore 'F'` each `!` would find an `F`
// beside it rather than another `!`, and no rule would ever match.  That is the same device the book
// uses, and the same one the older context-sensitive scene here needs.
//
// **A context naming parameters wants exactly that many.**  `!(a)` matches a cell carrying one
// number and would not match a bare `!`, which is the same rule the predecessor follows.  A context
// written as a plain letter names nothing and asks nothing -- which is what lets every
// context-sensitive L-system written before parameters existed go on working untouched.

context {
    parallel pixel scanner
    angles are degrees
    no gamma
    info {
        title 'A Signal Along a Filament'
        author 'J. Stephen Kress'
        description 'A parametric context passing a diffusing signal from cell to cell.'
        copyright true
        software __software__
    }
}

camera {
    location [0, 3.4, -7.2]
    look at [0, 0.25, 2.0]
    field of view 42
}

point light { location [7, 12, -11] }

point light {
    location [-8, 6, -7]
    color [0.28, 0.30, 0.36]
}

background [0.09, 0.10, 0.14]

// No ground here on purpose: this is a diagram of five numbers-in-a-row, and a plane under it
// contributes five shadows that read as five more rods.

// Twenty-one cells, thin but for one fat one in the middle.  A cell is a width and a step: the
// `!(...)` carries the signal and the `F(0.5)` is the length of cell it draws.
filament = lsystem {
    axiom '!(0.02)F(0.5)!(0.02)F(0.5)!(0.02)F(0.5)!(0.02)F(0.5)!(0.02)F(0.5)!(0.02)F(0.5)!(0.02)F(0.5)!(0.02)F(0.5)!(0.02)F(0.5)!(0.02)F(0.5)!(0.30)F(0.5)!(0.02)F(0.5)!(0.02)F(0.5)!(0.02)F(0.5)!(0.02)F(0.5)!(0.02)F(0.5)!(0.02)F(0.5)!(0.02)F(0.5)!(0.02)F(0.5)!(0.02)F(0.5)!(0.02)F(0.5)'

    // The steps are not cells, so they must not be counted as neighbours.
    ignore 'F'

    productions {
        '!(a)<!(c)>!(b)' -> '!(c + 0.25 * (a + b - 2 * c))'
    }

    controls {
        tubes
        length 0.5
    }
}

Cell = material {
    pigment [0.55, 0.78, 0.72]
    specular 0.40
    shininess 90
    ambient 0.22
}

// The same filament at five ages, laid one behind another.  Only `generations` differs.
lsystem filament { generations 0   material Cell  translate [-5.2, 0.35, 5.6] }
lsystem filament { generations 2   material Cell  translate [-5.2, 0.35, 3.8] }
lsystem filament { generations 6   material Cell  translate [-5.2, 0.35, 2.0] }
lsystem filament { generations 12  material Cell  translate [-5.2, 0.35, 0.2] }
lsystem filament { generations 20  material Cell  translate [-5.2, 0.35, -1.6] }
